\title{Proposition and Proof in Mathematics and Computer Science}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2025-09-05}
\meta{toc}{true}

\p{
    [Proof assistants](dhsorens-000B) are one link by which the notions of proposition and proof are
    relevant to computer scientists and mathematicians alike. Computer scientists use proof
    assistants, such as [Rocq](dhsorens-rocq) or [Lean](dhsorens-lean), to formally verify code
    whose security is paramount by stating and proving theorems. Mathematicians increasingly use
    proof assistants in a project to [formalize both classical and modern
    mathematics](dhsorens-000Z).
}

\p{
    However, proposition and proof for mathematicians and computer scientists often do not much
    resemble each other, both in how proofs are engineered as well as in the kinds of propositions
    formalized. Unless software specifications are explicitly mathematical, for example like in the
    case of [zero-knowledge proofs](dhsorens-002C), computer scientists typically formalize
    properties from a prose specification into a set of propositions and prove code correct with
    regards to that specification. These sorts of propositions comprising the specification are
    informed by experience and best practices, but [frequently not any formal, underlying
    theory](dhsorens-001H). In contrast, the propositions posed by mathematicians draw completely on
    the theory in which new propositions are couched, and the statements of propositions are derived
    in a methodical and highly rigorous manner.
    % Broadly speaking, I would characterize the mathematical and computer scientific approaches in
    % the following (deeply unrigorously derived) ways.
    These differences are, in my mind, well worth meditating on. My [PhD](dhsorens-phd) was in some
    ways an attempt to reconcile the mathematical approach to stating and proving theorems for [some
    specific software applications](dhsorens-000W); the jury is still out for me as to whether that
    is actually useful.
}

\p{
    We'll start this discussion is with proposition and proof in a purely mathematical context; from
    there, we can see the nuances of specification as we move into software at higher and higher
    levels of abstraction and involvement in daily, human life. My goal here is to explore the
    process of forming software specifications, arguing that rigorous software specification gets
    harder as the purpose of the software moves from mathematical computation to playing social,
    political, and economic roles in the world.
}

\transclude{dhsorens-001G} % proposition and proof for mathematicians 

\p{
    Crafting new definitions in mathematics is a strictly abstract and technical endeavor. The great
    advantage of doing work in mathematics is that the "world" in which one operates is extremely
    clearly defined and has virtually no ambiguities.
}

\p{
    When one goes to state and prove things about software, one finds one's self in a world that can
    be a lot less abstract, pure, and well-defined than the world of mathematics. This is precisely
    where the difficulty of proposition and proof relating to software comes into play.
}

\transclude{dhsorens-001H} % proposition and proof for computer scientists 


\transclude{dhsorens-001K} % the issue of incorrect specification -- proof assistants?